{"id":"06a55824-374a-4bda-828b-e4508d4a9358","object":"chat.completion","created":1785036498,"model":"deepseek-v4-flash","choices":[{"index":0,"message":{"role":"assistant","content":"{\n  \"implementation\": \"def solve(case):\\n    word_token_indices = case['word_token_indices']\\n    word_scores = case['word_scores']\\n    forced_words = case['forced_words']\\n    token_budget = case['token_budget']\\n    n = len(word_token_indices)\\n    forced_set = set(forced_words)\\n    forced_tokens = sum(len(word_token_indices[i]) for i in forced_set)\\n    if forced_tokens > token_budget:\\n        return {\\\"selected_words\\\": [], \\\"retained_token_indices\\\": [], \\\"tokens_used\\\": 0}\\n    remaining_budget = token_budget - forced_tokens\\n    selected = set(forced_set)\\n    candidates = [i for i in range(n) if i not in forced_set]\\n    candidates.sort(key=lambda i: (-word_scores[i], i))\\n    for i in candidates:\\n        need = len(word_token_indices[i])\\n        if need <= remaining_budget:\\n            selected.add(i)\\n            remaining_budget -= need\\n    selected_words = sorted(selected)\\n    retained_token_indices = []\\n    for idx in selected_words:\\n        retained_token_indices.extend(word_token_indices[idx])\\n    tokens_used = token_budget - remaining_budget\\n    return {\\n        \\\"selected_words\\\": selected_words,\\n        \\\"retained_token_indices\\\": retained_token_indices,\\n        \\\"tokens_used\\\": tokens_used\\n    }\"\n}","reasoning_content":"We need to implement solve(case) that takes a payload with fields: word_token_indices (list of lists of ints), word_scores (list of floats), forced_words (list of ints), token_budget (int). Possibly also _case_nonce but can ignore.\n\nWe need to produce an output object with:\n- retained_token_indices: list of integers (the indices of tokens that are kept? Actually the structural schema says \"retained_token_indices\" array of integers, \"selected_words\" array of integers, \"tokens_used\" integer. The description says \"Return words and tokens in original order with exact token usage.\" So \"selected_words\" is the indices of selected words (original order among selected ones). \"retained_token_indices\" might be the token indices that are kept? But careful: The output schema says retained_token_indices: array of integers, selected_words: array of integers. The task says \"Construct a compressed prompt from supplied keep probabilities, force-token constraints, sentence boundaries, and an exact global budget.\" Also \"Treat a word group as indivisible when accounting token cost.\" So we have words, each word has a list of token indices. We want to select a subset of words (respecting forced words) such that total number of tokens (sum of lengths of selected words' token groups) <= token_budget, and maximize sum of word_scores? But the description says \"Rank remaining words by descending supplied score and then source position.\" And \"Accept a word only when its entire token group fits the residual budget.\" So we're doing greedy selection: first add forced words (must be feasible). Then sort remaining words by descending score, then source position (which is index in original list? \"source position\" likely the original index). For ties, use index order (lower index first?). Then iterate and add if fits budget. Then output selected words in original order (since we need faithful reconstruction). Also tokens_used is the total number of tokens used. retained_token_indices: likely the set of token indices (from word_token_indices flattened) that are retained. But careful: \"retained_token_indices\" must be an array of integers, and \"selected_words\" array of integers (indices of selected words, in original order). The \"retained_token_indices\" should be the token indices that are kept, but they might not be in order? It says \"Return words and tokens in original order with exact token usage.\" So tokens should be in original order as they appear in the original sequence, but only those that belong to selected words. We need to produce retained_token_indices as the list of token indices from word_token_indices, in order as they appear in the original item, but only for selected words. That matches \"faithful reconstruction\": the token sequence is reconstructed in order. So we can flatten the word_token_indices for the selected words in original word order. That is what retained_token_indices should be.\n\nCheck output schema: \"retained_token_indices\" items type integer, array. \"selected_words\" items type integer (word indices), array. \"tokens_used\" integer.\n\nEdge cases: forced_words must be subset of word indices (0..n-1). If the total tokens of forced words exceed token_budget, we must reject? The description says \"Select forced words first and reject an infeasible forced set.\" So if forced_words consume more tokens than budget, we should return something? Probably we should return an object with empty arrays and tokens_used=0? But the problem says \"reject an infeasible forced set.\" In the task, it's an NLP task with hard constraints; if infeasible, maybe we should return empty? But the output must have required keys. Let's think: The description \"reject an infeasible forced set.\" Possibly we should raise an exception? But the function must return JSON object. Probably we should return an empty selection. However, in the fixtures, forced_words is always [0] and token_budget is enough (in first two cases budget=6, first word tokens=3, so feasible; second case budget=5, first word tokens=3, feasible; third case budget=5, first word tokens=3, feasible). So all feasible. The description says \"reject an infeasible forced set.\" That might mean we should not include any words if forced set can't fit. I'll assume we should return empty arrays and tokens_used=0 if forced set exceeds budget.\n\nAlso note word_scores are floats; use them directly.\n\nImplementation steps:\n\n1. Validate required fields exist: \"word_token_indices\", \"word_scores\", \"forced_words\", \"token_budget\". (Assume present)\n2. n = len(word_token_indices) = len(word_scores)\n3. forced_set = set(forced_words)\n4. Check if sum(len(word_token_indices[i]) for i in forced_set) > token_budget: then return {\"selected_words\": [], \"retained_token_indices\": [], \"tokens_used\": 0}\n5. Initialize selected = list(forced_set) (but we need to maintain order? Forced words are already a list; we should keep them in the order they appear in the original list? The final selected_words must be in original order. So we can have a boolean array or a set for selection, then later produce ordered list.\n6. remaining_budget = token_budget - sum(len(word_token_indices[i]) for i in forced_set)\n7. Create list of candidate indices not forced: indices = [i for i in range(n) if i not in forced_set]\n8. Sort indices by ( -word_scores[i], i ) . For descending score, then ascending index for tie-breaking.\n9. Iterate over sorted indices: for i in indices:\n   - tokens_needed = len(word_token_indices[i])\n   - if tokens_needed <= remaining_budget:\n        add i to selected set, remaining_budget -= tokens_needed\n   - else skip.\n10. After loop, produce selected_words list: sorted(selected) (original order) - since indices are integers, sorting gives ascending order which is original order because indices are sequential.\n11. tokens_used = token_budget - remaining_budget (or sum of lengths of selected words)\n12. retained_token_indices: concatenate word_token_indices[i] for i in selected_words (in that order).\n13. Return dict with keys \"selected_words\", \"retained_token_indices\", \"tokens_used\".\n\nNote: The output schema has additionalProperties: false, so no extra keys.\n\nNow write Python code. Use only standard library.\n\nThe solve function must be defined.\n\nTesting with fixtures manually? Not required.\n\nImplementation in JSON string.\n\nEnsure proper formatting.\n\nWrite code."},"logprobs"